window.EDITORIAL = {
  sources: [
    {id:'rt-taxonomy',title:'NASA · 2024 Technology Taxonomy',url:'https://www.nasa.gov/otps/2024-nasa-technology-taxonomy/',claim:'A structured technology map is broader than degree names. Used as a cross-check on the range of engineering fields.'},
    {id:'rt-ucsd-ae',title:'UC San Diego · Aerospace Engineering Specializations',url:'https://maeweb.ucsd.edu/undergrad/aero-eng-specializations',claim:'Current optional undergraduate emphases: Astrodynamics and Space Applications, Aerothermodynamics, and Flight Dynamics and Controls. Electives and timing require advance planning.'},
    {id:'rt-ucsd-me',title:'UC San Diego · Mechanical Engineering Specializations',url:'https://mae.ucsd.edu/undergrad/specializations',claim:'Controls and Robotics is an available ME specialization; it includes controls, embedded robotics, planning, and estimation options.'},
    {id:'rt-ucsd-curriculum',title:'UC San Diego · Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering catalog',url:'https://catalog.ucsd.edu/curric/MAE-ug.html',claim:'AE and ME share substantial engineering foundations while emphasizing different applications. Degree programs include design, computation, and laboratories.'},
    {id:'rt-isam',title:'NASA · In-Space Servicing, Assembly, and Manufacturing',url:'https://www.nasa.gov/isam/',claim:'The 2026 State of Play surveys capability areas, facilities, and developers. A research map, not a hiring forecast.'}
  ],
  routes:[
    {id:'robot',icon:'01 / RECOMMENDED',title:'Build the robots that build space',description:'Your strongest overlap: mechanisms, physical motion, spacecraft, assembly, and an expanding industrial frontier.',degree:'Aerospace BS, leaning toward Flight Dynamics and Controls; choose robotics and mechanical design projects deliberately.',depth:'Spacecraft mechanisms or robotic manipulation. Add dynamics, estimation, contact-aware control, and experimental validation.',proof:'A mechanism you designed, fabricated, measured, broke in a controlled test, and improved. Then a robot task with repeatable success criteria.',search:'robot',caution:'A robotics degree label is optional. Access to hardware projects and mentors matters. Space manipulation is a smaller market than robotics overall; preserve an Earth-based bridge.',fork:'If you love machining the joint more than predicting spacecraft motion, ME with Controls and Robotics may fit even better. If you love the coupled motion, stay close to aerospace dynamics.'},
    {id:'habitat',icon:'02 / STRONG PARALLEL',title:'Make space somewhere to live',description:'Vehicles get people there. Thermal systems, air, water, maintenance, and operability let them stay.',degree:'Aerospace or mechanical engineering; build depth in thermofluids, structures, controls, and systems integration.',depth:'Life-support hardware, habitat thermal control, leak detection, maintainable fluid systems, or human–robot operations.',proof:'A measured, benign flow/thermal experiment or simulated habitat inventory model, followed by supervised lab work on real hardware.',search:'habitat',caution:'Some ECLSS work needs chemistry, biology, or chemical engineering. Human habitats may remain a small customer market through the 2030s.',fork:'Choose between designing a subsystem, integrating the habitat, and operating it. These are different workdays even when they support the same mission.'},
    {id:'vehicle',icon:'03 / VEHICLES & FLIGHT',title:'Make extraordinary vehicles work',description:'Aircraft, reusable launch vehicles, reentry systems, and spacecraft. A direct expression of your aerospace interest.',degree:'Aerospace BS; compare Flight Dynamics and Controls, Astrodynamics and Space Applications, and Aerothermodynamics.',depth:'Pick one: guidance and navigation, propulsion, aerothermal analysis, structures, or flight test. Learn its interfaces.',proof:'Model a benign vehicle, compare predictions with a laboratory or club test, and explain the discrepancies.',search:'vehicle',caution:'Propulsion and flight hardware carry substantial facility and testing requirements. Much early work is analysis, drawings, reviews, and verification.',fork:'Ask which problem keeps your attention: where it goes, how it moves, what pushes it, or why it survives.'},
    {id:'crew',icon:'04 / A SEPARATE AMBITION',title:'Be one of the people who goes',description:'Build a valuable engineering career and pursue operational credibility, then compete for a scarce crew role.',degree:'A qualifying STEM route can work. Current NASA selection normally requires a STEM master’s or an accepted alternative plus experience.',depth:'Spacecraft operations, robotics operations, life-support systems, human factors/EVA, or test engineering can build relevant experience.',proof:'Demonstrated reliability in difficult team operations, technical judgment, communication, and mission-relevant expertise.',search:'operations',caution:'No specialty buys a seat. Medical qualification, citizenship, experience, selection timing, and the mission all matter. Commercial pathways remain uncertain.',fork:'Prefer a career you would still want if you never flew. Go to the Work in space page for the actual distinction between engineers and crew.'},
    {id:'ocean',icon:'05 / ANOTHER EXTREME WORLD',title:'Build machines for the deep ocean',description:'A real alternative and a bridge: remote robots, manipulation, inspection, navigation, and recovery.',degree:'ME, AE with relevant controls/fluids projects, ocean engineering, or EE for sensing and acoustics.',depth:'Underwater robotics, pressure-rated mechanical design, marine autonomy, acoustic navigation, or field reliability.',proof:'Start with simulation or supervised benign ROV work. Study navigation error, fault handling, and recoverability.',search:'underwater',caution:'The shared mathematics is useful, but pressure, buoyancy, corrosion, acoustics, and hydrodynamics require domain-specific expertise.',fork:'Choose ocean work because the environment excites you; it need not be a temporary consolation before space.'},
    {id:'iron',icon:'06 / TRANSLATE THE FANTASY',title:'Find the real “Iron Man” disciplines',description:'The cinematic suit bundles many professions. Decide which capability you actually want to invent.',degree:'AE for flight; ME/robotics for mechanisms and wearables; EE for sensing/power; materials for structural performance.',depth:'Human–machine interfaces, exoskeleton actuation, compact power and cooling, autonomy, flight control, or systems test.',proof:'A benign assistive device, passive mechanism, or simulator with measured limitations. A real engineering question beats a costume.',search:'human',caution:'A compact suit combining long endurance, extreme flight, armor, and weapons is not an established career specialty. Energy, heat, loads, and human tolerance remain constraints.',fork:'If the exciting part is protection and mobility, look at wearables and human factors. If it is the flying machine, look at aerospace vehicles. Defense is a customer domain, not a single technical skill.'}
  ],
  horizons: {
    '2034':{label:'Your first professional chapter',subtitle:'A career that works even if the Moon is late.',thesis:'Optimize for an entry point into actual hardware: spacecraft mechanisms, controls, manufacturing, thermal systems, integration, test, and flight operations. A 2033 graduation could lead to industry around 2034; graduate study could shift that.',likely:['Vehicles, satellites, aircraft, robots, and their supply chains continue to need qualified hardware and reliable operations. This is a robust engineering need, not a job-count forecast.','AI-assisted design and coding may change the work substantially. Learn to specify requirements, interrogate models, run experiments, and own decisions.','Space robotics and station systems are credible targets, but the number and timing of employers depend on funded programs.'],conditional:['Multiple sustained commercial stations and repeated servicing missions.','A growing lunar surface supply chain with recurring customers.','Larger-scale manufacturing in orbit with products customers will actually pay for.'],avoid:'Do not make your entire education depend on one station, one launch vehicle, or one company’s announced date.'},
    '2042':{label:'Depth, ownership, and leverage',subtitle:'You could be the engineer trusted with the subsystem.',thesis:'With roughly eight years after a 2034 start, a possible trajectory is owning mechanisms, leading a test campaign, developing autonomy, or integrating a complex vehicle. That timing is illustrative; responsibility follows evidence and opportunities.',likely:['Reliability, repairability, and lifetime cost become valuable wherever expensive hardware operates remotely.','The best niche may sit between fields: flexible spacecraft + robot contact; cryogenic fluid management + autonomy; habitat maintenance + human factors.','An experienced specialist who can communicate across disciplines can move toward technical leadership or founding a company.'],conditional:['Orbital yards assembling structures too large to launch in one piece.','Lunar construction equipment needing service, replacement parts, and operating crews.','Commercial research operators spending more time in orbit.'],avoid:'A busy rendering is not a market. Look for repeat contracts, operational uptime, revenue, and customers beyond demonstration funding.'},
    '2050':{label:'Civilization-scale possibility',subtitle:'Think in capabilities, not promised dates.',thesis:'By 2050 your career could span multiple technology cycles. Keep a deep technical center while learning new tools and adjacent systems. A mature off-Earth industrial economy is a scenario, not a scheduling assumption.',likely:['Energy, heat, materials, sensing, control, and maintenance still set physical constraints. Their implementation may look very different.','Large technical programs need people who connect physical evidence, design choices, operations, and economics.','Skills can migrate between Earth industry, ocean systems, aviation, and space.'],conditional:['Persistent lunar industry and more extensive human operations beyond low Earth orbit.','Robotic construction of large habitats, observatories, power systems, or propellant infrastructure.','Mars settlement support, orbital data-center economics, and other major new markets.'],avoid:'Do not treat asteroid mining, rotating cities, fusion drives, or fully self-replicating factories as guaranteed employment markets. Learn the enabling disciplines if they fascinate you.'}
  },
  math:[
    ['Arithmetic & algebra','Units, ratios, gear relations, force balances, mass budgets. The language that turns a sketch into a calculable machine.'],
    ['Geometry & trigonometry','Linkages, coordinate systems, sensor directions, CAD geometry, and the reach of a robot arm.'],
    ['Calculus','How position, velocity, heat, and flow change; how small contributions add into an entire system.'],
    ['Linear algebra','Rotating coordinate frames, estimating a vehicle’s state, and coordinating many joints or coupled variables.'],
    ['Differential equations','Predicting motion, vibration, temperature, and feedback over time. The bridge from a snapshot to behavior.'],
    ['Probability & computation','Quantifying noise, uncertainty, reliability, and whether measurements support the model. Numerical tools turn theory into usable predictions.']
  ],
  faq:[
    ['Does specializing automatically mean getting paid more?','No. Scarcity helps only when a customer needs the skill and can fund it. Level, responsibility, location, employer economics, equity, and program risk matter. An obscure specialty with three employers can be less secure than a transferable specialty with many. Senior systems engineers and technical leaders can be very highly valued.'],
    ['Will weapons development become redundant?','There is no sound basis to assume that. Threats, countermeasures, sensors, communications, manufacturing, cost, and reliability change. But you do not have to choose defense to do technically ambitious work. Your interest in space is enough reason to pursue it. This atlas treats defense as a professional application domain.'],
    ['Are humanoids the inevitable workers in space?','No. A humanoid may be useful where tools and layouts are built around people. Arms, rovers, free-flyers, and specialized mechanisms can be better when mass, energy, reliability, or environment dominates. Specialize in manipulation, mobility, autonomy, and hardware integration; those skills survive changes in the robot’s shape.'],
    ['Do I have to choose space versus ocean right now?','No. Dynamics, controls, mechanisms, materials, computation, and testing offer bridges. Later you will need to learn the environment seriously: vacuum is not deep-water pressure, and an underwater navigation problem is not an orbital one.'],
    ['Should I do a master’s or PhD?','A BS can lead to design, manufacturing, integration, test, and some controls jobs. A focused MS can deepen dynamics, robotics, thermofluids, or research experience. A PhD makes sense when you want to create new methods or knowledge and enjoy sustained research. It is not a universal entry ticket or pay upgrade. NASA astronaut eligibility is a separate reason to consider graduate qualifications.'],
    ['Will AI erase these jobs before I graduate?','No one can reliably forecast the 2034 task mix. AI may automate substantial modeling, code, drafting, and analysis. Build the ability to define the right test, recognize a faulty model, understand physical failure, work across interfaces, and take responsibility for evidence. These are useful capabilities; calling them AI-proof would be unjustified.'],
    ['Is this literally every possible aerospace specialization?','No finite list can be. This is a broad map of major disciplines and unusually relevant neighboring fields, with nested niches inside each. Employers combine and rename roles. Payload optics, atmospheric science, aviation certification, ground infrastructure, space medicine, and policy can each support entire additional maps.']
  ]
};
